Alloy Development From Sustainable Materials – Close-Loop Of Materials Using Ultrasonic Atomization
Jakub CIFTCIi1,2 *, Tomasz CHOMA1,2, Bartosz MOROŃCZYK1,2, Bartosz KALICKI1,2, Filip PUCHALSKI1 and Łukasz ŻRODOWSKI1
1AMAZEMET Sp. z o. o. [Ltd.], Al. Jana Pawła II 27, 00-867 Warsaw, Poland
2Warsaw University of Technology, Faculty of Materials Science and Engineering, Wołoska 141, 02-507 Warsaw, Poland
Abstract
Current methods for the production of spherical powders restrict the development of new chemical compositions dedicated to the additive manufacturing industry. This novel ultrasonic atomization process enables in-house powder production from even one gram up to a few kilograms to verify new alloys. The technology use vibrations to break the surface tension of liquid metal to allow atomization. To melt the material plasma torch or induction heating can be used allowing to process wide range of metals. Plasma-based ultrasonic atomization was used to manufacture spherical powder of a high-temperature alloy, while induction-based ultrasonic atomization was used to develop a low-melting alloy. Additional case study of recycling titanium alloy maching chips into the powder by arc melting and ultrasonic atomization is presented.
Keywords: Ultrasonic atomization, alloy development, additive manufacturing, custom powder production, recycling
Introduction
Metal powders are widely used in novel production methods, like laser powder bed fusion1), electron beam melting2), or direct energy deposition3). Additive manufacturing often requires materials with high flowability, and high packing density resulting in the need for spherical powders. The morphology limits the number of powder manufacturing methods that can be used for these processes. The AM industry mainly uses powders obtained by gas atomization4), water atomization5), and plasma/induction spheroidization6). These technologies allow for mass-production of powders. However, they are not conducive alloy development in AM due to costs associated with production changeover, requirements for specific feedstock types, and space requirements which do not allow the installation of such systems at research institutes or Universities. This results in the necessity to use powder blends7) or non-spherical powders8) influencing the homogeneity of chemical composition, and flowability.
Ultrasonic atomization solves this issue thanks to the usage of vibration to break the surface tension of liquid metal to form droplets that solidify in a free-flow manner9). The absence of a high-velocity fluid stream such as in gas or water atomization allows for scaling down the atomization chamber. The ability for the melting mechanism to produce liquid metal in the moment of contact with the sonotrode allows it to work with any feedstock type without interfering with alloy development due to the complex process of feedstock preparation, like powder10) or wire11). Ultrasonic atomization was successfully used to obtain custom powders with tailored chemical composition: Zr-based alloy for bulk metallic glass formation during laser powder bed fusion12), platinum-based superalloy Pt84-Al11-Cr3-Ru2 for high-temperature applications13), AZ31B magnesium alloy modified with lithium to reduce its density14), aluminum 5754 alloy for automotive industry15), as well as functional material like Ni-Mn-Ga Heusler alloy with magnetocaloric properties16).
C103 is a refractory-based niobium alloy for high-temperature applications like rocket propulsion systems and hypersonic missiles. The first use of this alloy was in Apollo Lunar Command Module rocket nozzles. Due to its main usage as thin-walled geometries and hard-to-machine nature, the examination of its usability in AM was launched. It was found that additively manufactured C103 can achieve properties at ambient and 1093°C temperature required by ASTM B655 and can surpass the ultimate tensile strength of its wrought counterpart17).
Magnesium alloys are extensively studied for orthopedic implants, which do not require removal thanks to their biodegradable properties without toxic ion release that can cause metallosis18). To adjust their corrosion rate to the bone tissue healing rate elements like silver can be used which additionally can result in an antibacterial effect of the lattice structures19).
Titanium alloys are commonly used in aviation and aerospace application due to their high strength to weight ratio. Close loop of such materials is critical for sustainable research, especially concerning their strategic usage.
In this article, ultrasonic atomization results of niobium C103 (Nb-10Hf-1Ti) alloy, magnesium alloy modified with silver and recycling of the titanium machining chips will be presented. *corresponding author, E-mail: jakub.ciftci@amazemet.com
