Experimental
Ultrasonic atomization was carried out on rePOWDER (Amazemet, Poland). C103 niobium alloy was atomized from narrow sheet
strips using a TZM rod sonotrode with a diameter of 30 mm which vibrated with a 40 kHz frequency. The ultrasonic vibrations,
delivered through a sonotrode, create a standing wave on the surface of the sonotrode, which is transferred to the molten metal. By
reaching the threshold amplitude, the internal forces of the liquid are broken, ejecting droplets of molten metal that solidify in an
argon atmosphere. The chamber in which atomization was conducted is presented in Figure 1.
Figure 1 Chamber of rePOWDER in which C103 alloy was atomized
The ultrasonic atomization of magnesium alloy was carried out using induction melting of the material in the crucible from which it
was poured by the over-pressure control of argon after lifting the sealing rod, which was closing the nozzle opening. The stream of
liquid metal contacts the 100 mm long vibrating blade operating at a frequency of 60 kHz. Due to the low density of magnesium
alloy which caused mist formation in the chamber (Figure 2A) induction-based ultrasonic atomization of copper alloy is presented in
Figure 2B to demonstrate the process. The phenomenon of ultrasonic atomization was described in detail in previous work9). Mg
alloy contained Zn whose boiling point is 1180 K, which is too low temperature to melt Mg-1%Ag. To avoid significant Zn
evaporation related to overheating the material to melt silver in the crucible master alloy was prepared using rePOWDER
(Amazemet, Poland) arc melting module. Measured masses of silver and Mg alloy rods were melted in the cold crucible
(water-cooled copper plate) using plasma. The decrease in weight was noticeable due to the evaporation of volatile elements however
for fast alloy screening own preparation of master alloy based on alloy with 25% Ag resulted in smaller deviations from theoretical
composition than using commercial MgAg master alloy, especially as a small amount was needed to obtain 1% silver in the final
powder. This had a beneficial effect on the sustainable consumption of available resources. The material after joining the matrix with
silver pellets prior to homogenization is presented in Figure 3A and the homogenized custom master alloy in Figure 3B.
