Induction-based ultrasonic atomization of the resultant magnesium alloy revealed the presence of submicron particles on the surface
of particles with desired particle size distribution for laser powder bed fusion (Figure 6). The formation of submicron particles might
be due to a melting temperature of 1223 K which causes evaporation of zinc and its deposition on the Mg alloy particles during
solidification and cooling stages22). The high temperature was required to obtain a stable flow of the material. This also caused
particle bonding. This phenomenon should be considered during the next trials. Using a nozzle with a larger diameter to maintain a
stable dosage of material on the atomizing plate might allow reducing the pouring temperature of the modified alloy.
Figure 6 Morphology of magnesium alloy powder modified with silver
Ti-alloy chips from CNC machine were placed in the cold crucible made from copper. Using plasma torch, they were melted into the
ingots (Figure 7A). Prepared feedstock was used for ultrasonic atomization using 40 kHz frequency. SEM observations revealed high
sphericity of the material (Figure B). Dynamic Image Analysis revealed very narrow particle size distribution. D10, D50, and D90
values are presented in table 1. Small amount of large particles are detected during volume – based analysis (Figure 8). Over 85 %
atomized powder is below 100 microns which is suitable fraction for laser powder bed fusion, electron beam melting, hot isostatic
pressing, and other powder-based manufacturing methods. The oxygen pickup during powder preparation was around 220 ppm
comparing to the feedstock (overall oxygen was below 600 ppm which meets the ASTM F2924 standard).
